.TH CTPOOL_FUTURE_CREATE_DETACHED 3 "2026-09-21" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ctpool_future_create_detached \- create a standalone future with no pool or queued task
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadpool.h>

ctpool_future *ctpool_future_create_detached(char **err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Allocates a future object that no cthread_pool task queue backs. An
outside producer, such as an engine driven by events that is not itself
a ctpool worker, must call ctpool_future_fulfill exactly one time to
deliver the result.

On success the future has a reference count of 2, which mirrors the
convention of ctpool_submit_future: the caller holds one reference,
which ctpool_future_free releases, and the producer holds the other,
which ctpool_future_fulfill releases. Each ctpool_future_get call that
runs on the future adds one more.

The library frees the struct once every reference has gone, in whatever
order they go. A call to ctpool_future_free can come before a call to
ctpool_future_fulfill, and either call can come while other threads
still wait in ctpool_future_get. This is exactly how a future that a
pool backs behaves.

This function always uses the standard malloc and free, not any custom
allocator, in the same way as the futures of ctpool_submit_future.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
err_str   Optional pointer to receive an error string on failure
          (pass NULL to ignore)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a new ctpool_future handle (not yet done) on success.
Returns NULL on allocation failure.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ctpool_future_fulfill (3),
.BR ctpool_future_get (3),
.BR ctpool_future_done (3),
.BR ctpool_future_free (3)
